123
00:10:32,142 --> 00:10:40,320
And you sense, you know, there was a black hat video where you guys walked through this and you're gonna put more out in the coming days, I think.

124
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:43,680
But walk me through that when you heard that that had happened.

125
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:44,772
What did you think?

126
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:47,172
Well, in the words of

127
00:10:48,334 --> 00:10:50,948
someone on our security team, it was definitely the

128
00:10:52,142 --> 00:10:54,052
Sort of most eye-opening

129
00:10:54,734 --> 00:10:58,084
security event that any of us were aware of.

130
00:10:58,414 --> 00:11:02,980
The way to think about it is that there's a lot of different lessons that we're taking away from it.

131
00:11:03,278 --> 00:11:09,156
But I think that it is a moment where we can, as a community, learn, we can understand what the shape of the future

132
00:11:09,902 --> 00:11:12,772
is. And to me, the salient points for the purpose of

133
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:23,140
how I'm thinking about it right now is that we had a model and we were evaluating it, and it managed to, it was in a sandbox that was well configured, and it managed to both

134
00:11:23,822 --> 00:11:27,840
break out of the sandbox and then also break into production infrastructure.

135
00:11:28,014 --> 00:11:29,252
It was intended to be hardened.

136
00:11:29,902 --> 00:11:33,120
And so that the capability there is quite high.

137
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:33,520
And

138
00:11:34,286 --> 00:11:39,040
there's some details about exactly how it all worked, and the fact that it was kind of not just a singular agent,

139
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:43,780
but really a swarm of agents that were focused on this goal and able to pass messages to each other.

140
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:53,892
And the sort of thing that you start to see is that with very capable agents, that there are limitations that human attackers have that slow us down

141
00:11:54,480 --> 00:12:03,844
that will no longer hold back the attackers, right? That they can look through lots of information through many different little vulnerabilities and chain them together into something significant.

142
00:12:04,334 --> 00:12:05,220
For me,

143
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:14,400
what I immediately started to realize and think about is what are the implications for six months from now when this kind of capability isn't just in the lab,

144
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:18,244
isn't just in a few places, but is really widely available.

145
00:12:18,702 --> 00:12:26,692
And I think that the answer is that every company needs to respond, needs to upgrade its security because attackers are going to fully automate.

146
00:12:27,054 --> 00:12:28,964
And you can even see with the release of

147
00:12:29,678 --> 00:12:35,076
GLM 5.3 that the cyber capabilities of open weight models available to everyone are going to increase.

148
00:12:35,662 --> 00:12:43,876
And I think that the important thing is that I actually think in the long run that defenders can win, that AI can actually make it so defenders can decisively win

149
00:12:44,110 --> 00:12:50,148
in a way that was never possible before, Because security always a cat and mouse game, attackers up level, defenders up level.

150
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:53,572
But AI can give defenders an unfair advantage.

151
00:12:54,158 --> 00:12:59,972
For example, formal verification of software, where you can actually prove that it is secure, mathematically prove security properties.

152
00:13:00,558 --> 00:13:06,596
The technology for this exists, but it is intractable for humans to actually do the work to prove those properties.

153
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:09,040
we have these models that are incredible at math, right?

154
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:18,240
They are solving, for example, Astra, our upcoming model, solved, we announced this a few weeks ago, solved 10 math problems, and this was with the $2,000

155
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:20,676
worth of compute that had been open for decades, right?

156
00:13:20,814 --> 00:13:25,092
Mathematicians had spent their whole careers on these problems, and now we have a model that can prove the answers.

157
00:13:25,614 --> 00:13:27,268
Those models definitely can prove

158
00:13:27,950 --> 00:13:28,580
formal

159
00:13:29,230 --> 00:13:31,040
safety properties of software.

160
00:13:31,374 --> 00:13:34,660
And so that's one place where unfair advantage for defenders.

161
00:13:34,990 --> 00:13:39,520
A second thing that we're doing is we're really investing in generating secure code.

162
00:13:39,918 --> 00:13:44,676
And you start to realize that as a human developer, there are many patterns that people just keep getting wrong.

163
00:13:44,878 --> 00:13:53,680
And I've been through this many times in my previous jobs. I was very focused on building secure infrastructure and just find like all these little things people do wrong.

164
00:13:54,062 --> 00:13:58,960
SQL injections people kind of know about, that there's little patterns, but there's lots of little anti-patterns as well.

165
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:07,120
For even like, for example, I remember it used to be like in Ruby that the regular expressions, there's like certain characters that if you get them wrong, that

166
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:13,760
there's just very common attacks where people can put a new line and it doesn't match, so you're able to add things to the payload.

167
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:20,100
All of those problems of just little security anti-patterns that you just keep having to tell your people don't do this, don't do this, don't do this.

168
00:14:20,974 --> 00:14:22,372
You can train your AI to never do it.

169
00:14:22,734 --> 00:14:26,596
And so I think that we actually have the ability to build the best security engineers in the world.

170
00:14:26,926 --> 00:14:27,908
And fundamentally

171
00:14:29,166 --> 00:14:32,228
the defenders can have the advantage,

172
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:35,908
but this is going to require a mindset shift.

173
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:37,732
This is going to require a mega project.

174
00:14:38,030 --> 00:14:42,276
This is something that the whole ecosystem needs to change how software is secured.

175
00:14:42,510 --> 00:14:47,556
Because there's a second thing that we've learned, which is that as these cyber models have gotten more capable,

176
00:14:48,270 --> 00:14:50,980
the number of bugs that they are finding in

177
00:14:51,662 --> 00:14:54,404
critical software, for example, the Linux kernel.

178
00:14:55,470 --> 00:14:57,088
They just keep finding more and more.

179
00:14:57,390 --> 00:15:09,540
And it really makes you realize that human written software, that the response is too slow. And so we need defenders to automate, to move at machine speed, because their adversaries certainly will.

180
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:14,688
What do you say to people who maybe and also they already have a negative kind of disposition of AI?

