00:16:17,998 --> 00:16:23,268
It should not be locked up in one place with one entity controlling it, because that's a different kind of risk.

196
00:16:24,238 --> 00:16:33,408
But it means that we do need to adapt, we do need to navigate through, and we need to use the information that we sort of painstakingly extracted from the future to see what is the shape of it.

197
00:16:33,742 --> 00:16:41,668
So to me, hugging face is a sort of proof point, a case study, a window into the future.

198
00:16:42,254 --> 00:16:50,308
It's not necessarily a future that any one company is creating, it's the overall economy, the overall set of actors in all these different countries who are building these kinds of models.

199
00:16:50,702 --> 00:16:52,740
And it shows you what will be possible and how

200
00:16:53,486 --> 00:16:54,820
attackers will be able to update.

201
00:16:55,054 --> 00:16:57,188
What it doesn't show you is how do defenders update.

202
00:16:58,030 --> 00:17:04,996
But that to me is what we are also trying to now show as well, that we can build these models that you can use to scan yourself.

203
00:17:05,328 --> 00:17:06,084
As an example,

204
00:17:07,008 --> 00:17:12,644
I asked Codex, and this is just off-the-shelf Codex, no special access, no special models.

205
00:17:12,878 --> 00:17:15,328
I just used my personal Codex to say, scan

206
00:17:15,328 --> 00:17:16,420
Gregbrockman.com.

207
00:17:16,718 --> 00:17:23,236
Tell me what vulnerabilities are there. And Gregbrockman.com is very simple. It's just a static site. I was like, come on, what's it gonna find?

208
00:17:23,854 --> 00:17:25,764
And it came back with like 13 findings.

209
00:17:26,382 --> 00:17:31,088
And each one of them, I was like, you know, I kind of knew about this, but it's so much work to fix it.

210
00:17:31,168 --> 00:17:38,128
And like, oh yeah, I kind of forgot that I left that open, or you know, here's some domain that was still, you know, it was legacy, I'm not using it anymore.

211
00:17:38,288 --> 00:17:42,848
I hadn't set the records that prevent people from spoofing your email address, a bunch of little things like that.

212
00:17:43,182 --> 00:17:44,208
That one seems like a big deal.

213
00:17:44,528 --> 00:17:46,436
Well, yeah, you're right. Now that you say it.

214
00:17:46,768 --> 00:17:47,684
Now, here's the thing.

215
00:17:48,302 --> 00:17:49,028
Honestly,

216
00:17:50,030 --> 00:17:54,688
to fix those kinds of things, you have to set up like DMARC to do it right, and there's this whole process you have to do roll.

217
00:17:54,848 --> 00:17:57,316
I'm just like, I don't know, it just takes time, it takes a lot of expertise.

218
00:17:57,614 --> 00:17:59,140
So instead I just asked Codex, can you fix it?

219
00:17:59,728 --> 00:18:03,408
And so it opened up my Cloudflare control panel and it just used computer use.

220
00:18:03,568 --> 00:18:06,180
It clicked around, it set a bunch of headers, changed a bunch of settings.

221
00:18:06,414 --> 00:18:11,556
I used to have it so that I had Cloudflare in front and then AWS on the back end, I was going over insecure HTTP here.

222
00:18:12,398 --> 00:18:18,010
Codex had figured that out. I actually am not even sure how it saw that because I thought everything was hidden behind Cloudflare.

223
00:18:18,350 --> 00:18:24,528
It actually was just like, you know what, I'm just gonna migrate you off of AWS. So it set up Cloudflare pages, moved over the content.

224
00:18:24,688 --> 00:18:27,168
I had an old jQuery version that was insecure.

225
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:32,100
It just got rid of jQuery entirely, and it was like 15 minutes to find all these findings.

226
00:18:32,430 --> 00:18:33,968
It was another hour to fix all of them.

227
00:18:34,128 --> 00:18:40,100
And for me, I just was the manager. I just gave it the intent. I said, what I want is I want to know where the problems are, and I want you to fix them.

228
00:18:40,558 --> 00:18:41,060
And it did it.

229
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:43,588
And that to me is more the future that we're promised.

230
00:18:44,128 --> 00:18:49,008
I want to hear, and I know this is more in the research world, but I think it's really relevant to everything.

231
00:18:49,550 --> 00:18:54,688
you talk about what you guys are doing now in response to Hugging Face.

