00:18:55,328 --> 00:19:01,924
one of your colleagues, Mia, told me the other day that I wish we had done a lot of the work we're doing before this happened.

233
00:19:02,528 --> 00:19:04,324
so what is that work that's happening now?

234
00:19:04,928 --> 00:19:13,088
So I would view the mode we've been in since the discovery of the hugging face incident as full incident response to up

235
00:19:13,248 --> 00:19:20,528
level every aspect of how we secure, monitor, and align our most capable models.

236
00:19:20,688 --> 00:19:20,848
Yeah.

237
00:19:21,134 --> 00:19:25,028
And we believe that models that are above a certain capability level, they require

238
00:19:25,678 --> 00:19:26,128
this. And

239
00:19:26,928 --> 00:19:27,248
the

240
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:32,260
It's been a very interesting technical problem to think about.

241
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:40,560
And it's not all us being caught flat-footed because I mean we anticipated this moment for quite some time.

242
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:45,540
If you look back at February, publicly we started talking about trusted access for cyber models, right?

243
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:53,412
That that was the first time we were really thinking about, okay, we've always had in our preparedness framework when cyber capabilities get to a certain level, you need to treat them differently.

244
00:19:53,678 --> 00:19:59,520
Now we're starting to think about the fact that these are going to be so transformative in the world. We need to think about how access is controlled.

245
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:09,360
Internally, we started to think about if we have very capable models that are able to break out of sandboxes, well, we need to make sure our sandbox is as industrial

246
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:17,120
grade, as you know, sort of foolproof as humanly possible, which really means engineering from the ground up around security properties.

247
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:27,364
And so we put some of our our um actually Mike Dalton, who was one of the people who gave that black hat talk, the task that he started to work on was build the most secure possible sandbox.

248
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:33,284
So we've been putting in place a bunch of infrastructure primitives and policy primitives for this moment.

249
00:20:33,550 --> 00:20:37,440
But it's also different when it actually hits, right? You never know when you're gonna hit that moment.

250
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:45,892
I think here we're actually very, I think it's we got to see this window in a very concrete way of okay, we're now at a serious capability level

251
00:20:46,414 --> 00:20:47,280
relative to

252
00:20:48,494 --> 00:20:50,160
the pieces we must act now.

253
00:20:50,638 --> 00:20:51,908
And so there's been a

254
00:20:52,686 --> 00:21:00,964
workflow shift that's been happening very rapidly because you start to think about a lot of our governance has been focused on deployment of model, but not necessarily the training of model.

255
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:02,820
Now we need to pull the governance back in.

256
00:21:03,182 --> 00:21:06,400
During the training and evaluation process, we also need good governance there.

257
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:13,060
We need to have standards around checks and balances around how do we know if it's secure enough, how do we know if it's aligned enough.

258
00:21:13,760 --> 00:21:21,732
And this alignment piece is actually very important because our belief is that if you make models extremely capable,

259
00:21:22,702 --> 00:21:26,756
you really want to make sure that as the capability grows, that

260
00:21:27,534 --> 00:21:32,720
their alignment grows with them, right? That you want those things to be in lockstep and sort of coordinated fashion.

261
00:21:32,800 --> 00:21:40,356
And so in that way, you kind of have this defense and depth of models that are trying to do the right thing, that you have a sandbox that's as secure as possible.

262
00:21:40,654 --> 00:21:49,200
and that I think that doing this in an iterative fashion, so that you're taking incremental jumps, so you can kind of understand as you go and make sure that your systems are

263
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:49,796
working.

264
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:56,260
all of that is very aligned with how we've thought about iterative deployment, how we've thought about the creation of this technology from the very beginning.

265
00:21:56,686 --> 00:22:01,476
So we are operationalizing that. And actually for me as a as a manager, as a

266
00:22:02,190 --> 00:22:07,876
sort of thinking about the organizational system for producing this technology, it's also been a very important challenge

267
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:11,200
because this means you now need different people in the room to make decisions.

268
00:22:11,360 --> 00:22:16,160
You need a different process for how do you know what the requirements are, how do you execute against those priorities,

269
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:21,412
if there's a change in your technical understanding, how do you propagate that, how do you make sure people are in the right rooms?

270
00:22:21,838 --> 00:22:26,240
But again, in some ways it's been a phase shift, but it's been one we've been preparing for.

271
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:35,236
earlier this year, I spent a lot of effort to really try to bring together research and security to start having, we started doing, for example, these weekly

