384
00:32:33,294 --> 00:32:38,692
Like if you look back at kind of these different models that have been released over time and their performance on different benchmarks,

385
00:32:39,470 --> 00:32:42,148
it's just you know, sort of that that exponential.

386
00:32:42,734 --> 00:32:50,704
And so I think that being said, the rest of the answer is that if you go for if we go forward two years and we look back, I think that

387
00:32:51,104 --> 00:32:53,104
we will say this is when AGI was created.

388
00:32:53,422 --> 00:32:54,704
So I think we are going through that moment.

389
00:32:55,024 --> 00:32:58,084
But it's not it's not something where you could just say it now.

390
00:32:58,478 --> 00:33:01,572
I don't think that it's ever going to be as simple as you can say this was the date.

391
00:33:01,774 --> 00:33:11,012
There's so many axes. And I think that maybe one lesson in general to the whole AGI conversation and why it's so complicated is because humans, I think, are capable of much more

392
00:33:11,504 --> 00:33:14,084
than a naive view would give credit to.

393
00:33:14,384 --> 00:33:16,644
I think that the diversity of what we do,

394
00:33:17,518 --> 00:33:22,724
how we set goals, how we accomplish goals, how tasks work, how jobs work, all these things extremely deep.

395
00:33:23,342 --> 00:33:33,744
And so I think that what we're seeing is that there's different aspects of either how people accomplish tasks or limitations that are hard for humans to overcome.

396
00:33:33,824 --> 00:33:40,784
like the thing I mentioned with read all the code, where these AIs are becoming able to fill in those gaps, are able to do more and more,

397
00:33:41,024 --> 00:33:48,544
and that humans, it turns out that there are some things that we also really find are extremely important for it to be a human. there.

398
00:33:48,944 --> 00:33:50,544
where does the judgment come from ultimately?

399
00:33:50,798 --> 00:33:53,824
Where does the taste come from? Who decides what's good and what's not good?

400
00:33:54,224 --> 00:33:56,064
that is something that should be uniquely human.

401
00:33:56,430 --> 00:33:57,828
And I'll tell you just to give you an example,

402
00:33:58,670 --> 00:34:00,964
one thing we've seen with image generation.

403
00:34:01,344 --> 00:34:05,604
When you just generate an image with ChatGPT and you show it to someone, no one cares.

404
00:34:06,126 --> 00:34:07,812
It is uninteresting to anyone besides you.

405
00:34:08,144 --> 00:34:11,587
But if you take a photo of your family and you say, turn this into

406
00:34:11,587 --> 00:34:16,864
a caricature, turn this into a cartoon style, people love it.

407
00:34:17,102 --> 00:34:17,764
It goes viral.

408
00:34:18,318 --> 00:34:22,304
And so there's something about that element of humanity that matters so deeply.

409
00:34:22,574 --> 00:34:24,132
People want to connect with other people.

410
00:34:24,430 --> 00:34:32,544
And that is actually, in my mind, the purpose of this technology, a thing that I think is really emerging is for deepening human connection.

411
00:34:32,718 --> 00:34:41,220
So you can spend more time with your family, you can spend more time with people you want to spend time with, less time, typing away at a machine, less time, getting carpal tunnel,

412
00:34:41,584 --> 00:34:42,276
the whole thing.

413
00:34:42,574 --> 00:34:50,544
And so I think that there is something about what AGI is as it's starting to unfold. It's almost this fog that's lifting.

414
00:34:50,734 --> 00:34:53,636
It's almost teaching us more about ourselves and what it means to be human.

415
00:34:54,510 --> 00:35:01,828
Given the capabilities that you guys have internally and the measures you're taking now, moving compute to alignment and putting in all these checkpoints,

416
00:35:03,470 --> 00:35:06,532
how are you gonna know when you're in a good spot there?

417
00:35:07,024 --> 00:35:12,228
and that you've solved the problems and the models are aligned?

418
00:35:13,006 --> 00:35:15,424
So I think the perspective here is very critical.

419
00:35:15,744 --> 00:35:22,692
this is something we've again spent lots of time over the years thinking about, debating, really trying to refine a perspective.

420
00:35:23,150 --> 00:35:23,904
In my view,

421
00:35:24,878 --> 00:35:26,336
It really comes down to iteration.

422
00:35:26,478 --> 00:35:29,220
It comes down to iterative deployment, iterative development.

423
00:35:29,456 --> 00:35:35,456
So you need to have checks and balances, you need to have evaluations, you need to have bars, you need to have all of these things, you need to have governance,

424
00:35:35,536 --> 00:35:38,628
you need to have a setup that is technically sound.

425
00:35:39,296 --> 00:35:46,788
And the way to think about it is everything is gradual, everything is incremental, and you learn and you update, you iterate.

426
00:35:47,086 --> 00:35:53,056
And that as the model capabilities increase, the tightness of those bounds has to increase correspondingly.

427
00:35:53,294 --> 00:35:55,856
And so think of it a little bit like if you're launching a rocket.

428
00:35:56,210 --> 00:36:03,616
right? If the rocket's very small, like there's a lot of things you can probably do wrong, and it's fine, right? And you just kind of like launch it and like you know, it's all good.

429
00:36:03,950 --> 00:36:07,044
Rocket bigger, the tolerance is much more tiny.

430
00:36:07,150 --> 00:36:12,004
You know, you go from you know multiple centimeters to multiple millimeters, that's where you have to be.

431
00:36:12,526 --> 00:36:21,456
And as you start to add more responsibility to what the payloads are for the rocket, where it's going, what it's doing, you think about all the things that are relying on that rocket and how

432
00:36:21,806 --> 00:36:24,004
all the systems that have to go right, all the things that go wrong.

433
00:36:24,526 --> 00:36:33,732
You have to continue to ratchet your ability to be able to measure and to ensure that you're able to even do the engineering in addition to the safety and security properties of it.

434
00:36:34,286 --> 00:36:40,292
And that is very much how we think about it is that there are moments where we do introduce very punctuated change.

435
00:36:40,910 --> 00:36:43,428
When we made the preparedness framework the first time was an example of that.

436
00:36:43,982 --> 00:36:48,900
But even the preparedness framework, we very carefully, very deliberately baked in the ability to change it.

437
00:36:49,136 --> 00:36:52,292
We said there will be new information, we will have to learn, we will have to update.

438
00:36:52,590 --> 00:36:59,136
But at any point, we wanted to be clear what are our bars, how do we handle it when a model reaches a certain level of capability?

439
00:36:59,502 --> 00:37:01,924
And we recently talked about the fact that Astra

440
00:37:02,702 --> 00:37:04,516
is approaching cybercritical.

441
00:37:04,896 --> 00:37:07,396
It's not necessarily there because

442
00:37:08,814 --> 00:37:11,716
in order to really prove it requires evals that aren't fully

443
00:37:12,398 --> 00:37:19,056
that you know that aren't fully defined, but that we are saying, okay, it's starting to approach it, so let's treat it as if it is.

444
00:37:19,150 --> 00:37:25,412
And so then that means we need to go and execute on certain safeguards and certain checks and balances, and you're seeing all of that play out.

445
00:37:25,710 --> 00:37:30,020
And so I think that the answer to your question is that we define a bar, we hit the bar,

446
00:37:30,736 --> 00:37:30,896
you

447
00:37:31,696 --> 00:37:35,216
have to take a framework that looks that way, right? To really focus on the iteration.

448
00:37:35,470 --> 00:37:43,776
Are you learning and are the bars that you're setting tightening in a sufficiently sort of in a in a sufficiently executed way

449
00:37:44,206 --> 00:37:53,060
in order to deliver the properties that are required? Yeah, I mean your rocket analogy is an interesting one because rockets also explode a lot. And I've been thinking about

450
00:37:53,710 --> 00:37:57,296
Ural's concern as these things get more capable.

451
00:37:57,998 --> 00:38:05,056
yeah, the concern of controlling them and how you can, and whether there's kind of like a Pandora's box situation that's unfolding right now.

452
00:38:05,216 --> 00:38:13,252
And I don't know if you've thought about that personally recently, with the insights you guys have on even what's ahead of Astra and what's causing you to make this slowdown.

453
00:38:13,776 --> 00:38:15,856
Um but yeah, do you worry about that?

454
00:38:16,096 --> 00:38:24,496
We view it as our job to really think through the implications of this technology. that is why we're here, is that we want to help steer it in a more positive direction than it would without us.

455
00:38:24,590 --> 00:38:28,132
Like that that that is that is table stakes for our mission, that is how we approach things.

456
00:38:28,686 --> 00:38:30,656
And I think it is important to

457
00:38:31,936 --> 00:38:34,500
take two two lenses to what you just said.

458
00:38:35,118 --> 00:38:36,708
The first one is that,

459
00:38:37,454 --> 00:38:40,256
again, this technology is not being created in a vacuum, right?

460
00:38:40,336 --> 00:38:47,620
That there are many people, many actors, many companies, many organizations, many countries even, that are pursuing the creation of this technology.

461
00:38:48,014 --> 00:38:54,532
And we happen to be in a spot where we have some lead, we have some ability to see into that future.

462
00:38:55,456 --> 00:39:01,348
And it is both important to think about how do we develop responsibly, safely, and securely.

463
00:39:01,806 --> 00:39:03,296
That's something that we spend a lot of time on.

464
00:39:03,502 --> 00:39:07,056
You can see it very concretely, how we prioritize that in everything we do.

465
00:39:07,406 --> 00:39:09,764
And I think that actually the mistakes we've made in the past is actually

466
00:39:10,478 --> 00:39:16,740
talking less about the fact that this is how we operate, that safety is such a first class priority, first class requirement.

467
00:39:18,190 --> 00:39:21,380
Just doing it, but not necessarily talking about it as publicly.

468
00:39:21,838 --> 00:39:30,176
And I think that that's something we're starting to realize that we do need to help people understand that that is how that is a core value of ours. That's something we care about so deeply.

469
00:39:30,496 --> 00:39:35,776
I think we've always been the field leader in safety, security, alignment, but I don't think people have necessarily known that.

470
00:39:36,016 --> 00:39:40,816
Yeah, even with the noise Anthropic makes about how they're the leader in that capacity, you think you guys have been?

471
00:39:41,136 --> 00:39:43,396
I believe at a substantive level that we have been.

472
00:39:43,776 --> 00:39:50,500
And do you think that what you guys are doing and the capabilities you're seeing and needing to redo you know the preparedness framework and all that implies

473
00:39:51,150 --> 00:39:56,256
you are actually just way ahead of maybe where everyone thought you were relative to Anthropic and the other Frontier Labs?

474
00:39:56,416 --> 00:40:02,660
Like, does this suggest that you all have a breakthrough internally that the world hasn't seen?

475
00:40:03,136 --> 00:40:05,696
Well, I can't comment on how,

476
00:40:06,606 --> 00:40:08,356
you know, on competitive positioning perhaps.

477
00:40:08,576 --> 00:40:17,668
But I guess I would just say that in my mind, we're very focused on the work that we're doing, and that we think that it we see smooth scaling, and I think that the exponential continues.

478
00:40:17,998 --> 00:40:22,256
And so it's a little bit less about where are we relative to others, a little bit more about the way we look at it is where are we

479
00:40:22,416 --> 00:40:25,988
relative to expectation or what do we think we will be able to accomplish.

480
00:40:26,350 --> 00:40:27,012
And I think that

481
00:40:27,726 --> 00:40:36,656
the reason this matters is that the bar that we hold ourselves to doesn't really change depending on kind of what the ecosystem is showing, because

482
00:40:36,816 --> 00:40:39,108
there's so much noise out there. It's hard to know what's real.

483
00:40:39,374 --> 00:40:45,764
That what we focus on is like, are we doing right by the our own conscious, by our own standards, by what we believe is important?

484
00:40:46,256 --> 00:40:48,324
And that is the number one lens that we take.

485
00:40:48,686 --> 00:40:57,456
And the second thing I wanted to add is that I think that the we, you know, there's this recent picture that came out of, or this this open letter called Pacing the Frontier.

486
00:40:57,536 --> 00:40:58,788
We talked about how we support that.

487
00:40:59,056 --> 00:41:01,924
And the the the language in there is very deliberately chosen.

488
00:41:02,336 --> 00:41:12,496
Pacing is a very important word, right? That if you think about what's happening, is that what we're saying is that you can't take a just like one-dimensional view to what it means to deliver a model.

489
00:41:12,690 --> 00:41:18,436
right? If you just look at capability, you don't look at alignment, you don't look at the kind of safety properties, security properties of how it was trained.

490
00:41:18,926 --> 00:41:19,844
It's too myopic.

491
00:41:20,096 --> 00:41:23,936
At this level of capability, you need to think about those as first cost requirements.

492
00:41:24,046 --> 00:41:32,416
And in fact, they are the bottleneck, right? Because those are the areas that I think people have sort of are, you know, have just focused on less in an integrated fashion.

493
00:41:32,750 --> 00:41:34,656
And that integration, by the way, critical, right?

494
00:41:34,736 --> 00:41:40,836
That I think that where people go wrong is when they think that you can separate out these properties, you can have just a safety, you can have just a capability.

495
00:41:41,262 --> 00:41:51,396
No, it's one thing. We're trying to deliver one system, one AGI that uplifts you, that is helpful to you, that delivers on your goals, that you can steer, that is aligned with what you want.

496
00:41:51,630 --> 00:41:52,932
Like that is what we are delivering.

497
00:41:53,326 --> 00:41:58,884
And so that backflows to all these questions of bars and testing and evaluations, safety, security.

498
00:41:59,758 --> 00:42:01,856
all of that is very, very critical.

499
00:42:02,256 --> 00:42:09,220
I think I'll pivot us a little bit because I want to hear you talk about the merge and what's going on with that.

500
00:42:09,456 --> 00:42:19,332
So we've been talking about the existential risk, you know, what's happening at the frontier, which I think most people in the world don't see, right? That's happening inside companies like OpenAI.

501
00:42:20,046 --> 00:42:24,388
What people do see is how chat is evolving, how codex is evolving.

502
00:42:24,654 --> 00:42:33,252
And one of your big priorities in this role, as I understand it, is what you guys call the merge internally, which is creating the super app, this combined ChatGPT and Codecs.

503
00:42:33,936 --> 00:42:36,996
And you've got ChatGPT for work now and the and the tabs.

504
00:42:37,616 --> 00:42:46,016
but at the same time, like it doesn't feel like that's the end state, you know, and I'm a user of it, and I see brittleness in how it's currently executed.

505
00:42:46,096 --> 00:42:47,076
Some things don't sync.

506
00:42:47,406 --> 00:42:49,188
You can tell you're still combining tech stacks.

507
00:42:49,870 --> 00:42:54,532
And I'm curious, once you're done with that work, what is the end product for people that you imagine?

508
00:42:55,214 --> 00:42:56,176
A personal AGI.

509
00:42:56,654 --> 00:42:57,216
Personal AGI.

510
00:42:57,456 --> 00:42:59,616
And how does that manifest as software?

511
00:42:59,936 --> 00:43:00,164
Yeah.

512
00:43:02,208 --> 00:43:09,188
It's a really, really exciting vision in my mind, right? Because where we started with ChatGPT, it was a text box, you could ask anything.

513
00:43:10,126 --> 00:43:12,932
But if you think about it, it was limited in a very fundamental way.

514
00:43:13,328 --> 00:43:16,228
The only thing it could do is output some text for you.

515
00:43:16,590 --> 00:43:19,204
The only impact it could have in the world was, kind of in

516
00:43:19,982 --> 00:43:23,088
your brain, right? That it wasn't able to look at your calendar.

517
00:43:23,278 --> 00:43:24,964
It wasn't able to look at your email.

518
00:43:25,550 --> 00:43:27,460
And all of that is now changed.

519
00:43:28,398 --> 00:43:35,780
ChatGPT work is really, by the way, a codex harness in the cloud, right? So we had chat that was this question and answer system

520
00:43:36,688 --> 00:43:43,888
iterated turns. People actually then go beyond just quick questions to health, like all these kinds of queries that are able to help them.

521
00:43:44,334 --> 00:43:47,140
We had Codex, which was an agentic system that can go do work.

522
00:43:47,888 --> 00:43:51,168
And it was so obvious it's like chat has to become agentic.

523
00:43:51,488 --> 00:43:57,028
And so the place we started at the beginning of the year was to say, well, let's have a great chat. Let's you know make it also do agent stuff.

524
00:43:57,422 --> 00:44:00,048
And it just turned out to be so hard to execute.

525
00:44:00,302 --> 00:44:06,528
Because now we have two agentic stacks, and then we compare between one and the other, and there'd be something that would work in this one, and we'd be like, why is it working in that one?

526
00:44:06,798 --> 00:44:07,812
And it was just so slow.

527
00:44:08,206 --> 00:44:14,372
And so the decision we made instead is to say, let's take the agentic stack we have, the codex stack, and bring it to ChatGPT.

528
00:44:14,798 --> 00:44:18,688
And so what you see with chat work is exactly right. This is an intermediate

529
00:44:18,688 --> 00:44:22,288
state. And in some ways, it's kind of funny if you think about it.

530
00:44:22,606 --> 00:44:28,288
ChatGPT was a text box, ChatGPT work is a text box. But this text box is way more awesome. It can be way more thick.

531
00:44:29,168 --> 00:44:29,568
Exactly.

532
00:44:29,902 --> 00:44:31,396
And so where we want to be is you should,

533
00:44:32,302 --> 00:44:32,964
it should be simple.

534
00:44:33,294 --> 00:44:34,448
User should not have to think about this.

535
00:44:34,528 --> 00:44:38,980
You shouldn't have to think about tabs or switching, and you shouldn't have model pickers, you shouldn't have thinking levels.

536
00:44:39,278 --> 00:44:40,260
You should have an AGI.

537
00:44:40,622 --> 00:44:41,508
You should be able to ask it.

538
00:44:41,806 --> 00:44:43,108
And in fact, you shouldn't even have to ask.

539
00:44:43,406 --> 00:44:48,768
It should proactively say, hey, I noticed that your favorite band, because it knows you well, is in town.

540
00:44:49,070 --> 00:44:50,048
Tickets went on sale.

541
00:44:50,254 --> 00:44:53,348
I got you the tickets exactly where you want for you and your family.

542
00:44:53,808 --> 00:44:59,088
And I only had 10 minutes to do it because everything is going to go so fast, so I just purchased it proactively, hope that's okay.

543
00:44:59,248 --> 00:45:06,288
And if you've built enough trust with the agent, so you've given permission to do that, and it kind of knows that, well, these tickets are within your price range, within your budget.

544
00:45:06,414 --> 00:45:08,900
These other ones, oh no, no, I got to get permission for that.

545
00:45:09,408 --> 00:45:10,848
That's the kind of system that we want.

546
00:45:10,928 --> 00:45:12,740
So trust is the key word.

547
00:45:13,070 --> 00:45:21,408
EQ, also very important, that if you've shared some information, like you've talked about a health condition, maybe that's okay information for the AI to talk about with a parent,

548
00:45:21,488 --> 00:45:23,876
but maybe it's not okay to talk about with a stranger.

549
00:45:24,622 --> 00:45:33,008
All of that judgment and also observability and really helping you build up that trust relationship with the AI so that you know what

550
00:45:33,582 --> 00:45:37,700
you know when it's operating on your behalf that'll pursue your goals, that's very, very critical.

551
00:45:38,254 --> 00:45:47,204
But the same thing, same kind of technology, the underlying AGI stack, one stack, one way of doing connectors so it's able to hook up to this context, also applies to the enterprise.

552
00:45:47,694 --> 00:45:52,368
And we're really seeing it not just for software engineering, which by the way, has been totally transformed this year.

553
00:45:52,590 --> 00:45:56,900
And to the point of AGI being a gradual thing or a thing where we just constantly update our expectations.

554
00:45:57,230 --> 00:46:00,128
Like we all kind of forget what software was one year ago. Sure.

555
00:46:00,448 --> 00:46:01,060
Totally different.

556
00:46:01,568 --> 00:46:07,168
A very interesting thing within OpenAI is that we're at basically 100% adoption of ChatGPT work across every function, right?

557
00:46:07,248 --> 00:46:08,804
Sales, finance, everyone.

558
00:46:09,168 --> 00:46:14,500
And whenever we've rolled a new model and it goes away, or you know, the system breaks for whatever internal reason,

559
00:46:15,758 --> 00:46:17,808
everyone's so upset, they're I cannot do my work.

560
00:46:18,190 --> 00:46:23,968
And you're like, I don't get it. Six months ago, you didn't have this, and you're doing your work just fine. Like, why are you, you know, why are you complaining?

561
00:46:24,142 --> 00:46:28,132
And you realize people adapt so quickly and learn how to get the most out of these tools.

562
00:46:28,608 --> 00:46:32,368
And once you're there, you also just sort of forget that it was any other way.

563
00:46:32,654 --> 00:46:39,888
And so there's something about this where I think we will have these incredible, amazing tools, these assistants that will remove so much of the toil,

564
00:46:40,142 --> 00:46:46,768
that will mean that you can spend your time doing what you want to do, that you can focus on building a better life, you can focus on if you want to be making more money,

565
00:46:46,848 --> 00:46:53,008
if you want to be relaxing more, if you want to spend more time with your family, any of those things, you will have the agency to do it. You will have the agency to build.

566
00:46:53,134 --> 00:46:58,148
If you have a vision, you have creativity, you want it to come into the world, you will have the tools to do that.

567
00:46:58,528 --> 00:47:01,828
So I think that what ChatGBT will do is help you

568
00:47:02,510 --> 00:47:06,820
with what you want to accomplish. If you want to make your world better, that is what chat will help you accomplish.

569
00:47:07,168 --> 00:47:15,556
And the implications of that are that what the coding AI tools have done to engineering, this could do to all knowledge work, all kinds of fields.

570
00:47:15,808 --> 00:47:23,088
And that's how you guys used to talk about AGI is like, you know, it was basically AI that could do most economically viable work better than humans,

571
00:47:23,168 --> 00:47:25,988
and what you're describing now in the product kind of sounds like that.

572
00:47:26,574 --> 00:47:30,628
I think that the amplification we see in software engineering, we expect across every field for sure.

573
00:47:30,990 --> 00:47:32,964
And there is something that I think that we

574
00:47:33,966 --> 00:47:37,316
It is also worth really thinking about, which is the software engineers,

575
00:47:38,094 --> 00:47:43,872
rather than having more vacation time and having more chill job, they're all working harder than ever before.

576
